    {
      "id": "tiktok-7710295660251188502",
      "number": 8,
      "title": "Your to-do list isn't too big. You're just seeing all of it at once. You had the same eight tasks as everyone else. You just spent the whole morning staring at the pile while it got heavier. Many ADHD brains take in a task list as one huge block instead of separate steps. The longer you look at it, the bigger it feels, so you freeze. That's not laziness. It's overwhelm. So stop looking at the pile. Pick the smallest thing on it, something you can finish in two minutes, like replying to one email, and do only that. The pile starts shrinking the second your hands are busy. Same pile. One at a time. ADHD or regular? Tell me which one you are 👇",
      "caption": "Your to-do list isn't too big. You're just seeing all of it at once. You had the same eight tasks as everyone else. You just spent the whole morning staring at the pile while it got heavier. Many ADHD brains take in a task list as one huge block instead of separate steps. The longer you look at it, the bigger it feels, so you freeze. That's not laziness. It's overwhelm. So stop looking at the pile. Pick the smallest thing on it, something you can finish in two minutes, like replying to one email, and do only that. The pile starts shrinking the second your hands are busy. Same pile. One at a time. ADHD or regular? Tell me which one you are 👇",
